Third-party code

Nothing is vendored into this repository: every file here is covered by the
licence above. A linked `spacebar` binary is not, and a distributed binary has
to carry these licences with it. `go.mod` is the authority; this is the same
list spelled out.

    github.com/spf13/cobra                Apache-2.0
    github.com/spf13/pflag                BSD-3-Clause
                                          Copyright (c) 2012 Alex Ogier
                                          Copyright (c) 2012 The Go Authors
                                          (indirect, reached through cobra)
    github.com/inconshreveable/mousetrap  Apache-2.0
                                          (indirect, reached through cobra, and
                                          linked only in a Windows build)

One direct dependency, and two indirect. Each licence text is in the module's
own source, which `go mod download` fetches and `go.sum` pins, and the full
text of all three is in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES so that it travels with a
distribution that is one executable and nothing else. `spacebar licenses`
prints that file from inside the binary.

SPEC.md §3.1 permits four more, each named with the reason it earns its place,
and no others. A pull request that would add a sixth direct requirement has to
argue for it. This list grows as the milestones land; it does not grow
otherwise.

This list is asserted rather than maintained by hand:
internal/lint/notice_test.go holds it to THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES, which is
generated across linux, darwin, and windows on both architectures. That
generation is the point. mousetrap is behind `//go:build windows`, so a scan
run on the maintainer's machine does not see it at all, and the Windows archive
would have shipped without its notice. That is exactly the failure this file
exists to prevent, arrived at by the route nobody checks.

No dependency ships a NOTICE file of its own, so Apache-2.0 §4(d) has nothing
further to propagate here. If one ever does, its NOTICE is reproduced in full
below this line, and the same test fails until it is.

Everything else this tool needs is the Go standard library. `make vuln` runs
govulncheck and fails closed, so a build that cannot be checked is a build that
does not pass.
